Tearing down an AKS Cluster safely is like closing down our digital shopping mall for good. You don't just dynamically bulldoze the building while shops are still open and vaults are still locked inside! If you just delete the cluster immediately, Azure might leave behind "orphaned" items—like expensive Load Balancers, Public IP addresses, or unattached hard drives—that keep charging your credit card every month.
Here is how a safe, step-by-step Decommission Script (decommission-aks.sh) works using our mall analogy:
 * Step 1: Evict the Stores & Security Desk (Helm Releases & Deployments): First, we check who is inside the data, identity, platform-foundation, and istio-ingress floors. When you say "yes," we cleanly shut down the stores and the Istio front security desk so Azure unhooks the cloud Load Balancer properly.
 * Step 2: Empty the Fireproof Vaults (Persistent Volumes & Disks): Remember how we put a Retain safety lock and a helm.sh/resource-policy: keep sticker on your PostgreSQL and Keycloak vaults? Because of that safety lock, uninstalling Helm won't delete your data. This step shows you every vault still sitting on the floor and asks if you want to permanently shred them or leave them safe in Azure.
 * Step 3: Release the Street Address (Static Public IP): We check the permanent street address (Public IP) reserved for Istio and ask before releasing it back to Azure.
 * Step 4: Demolish the Mall Building (The AKS Cluster): We list the cluster and its node wings (systempool and userpool) and ask for confirmation before tearing down the virtual machines.
 * Step 5: Tear Up the Parking Lot & Land (VNet & Resource Group): Finally, we check the private road network (VNet) and the main Azure folder (Resource Group) to make sure zero leftover cloud parts remain.
