The Interactive Decommission Script (decommission-aks.sh)
Best Practices Built Into This Script
 * Live Inventory Inspection: Before every single step, the script runs kubectl, helm, or az commands to show you the exact live Deployments, Services, PVCs, Disks, and IPs that are about to be affected.
 * Granular Step-by-Step Confirmation: You can type y to approve a step, or n to skip it (for example, if you want to delete the apps and Istio to save compute money, but keep the PostgreSQL disks and VNet intact).
 * Retained Disk Cleanup: Because our StorageClass uses reclaimPolicy: Retain, deleting a Kubernetes PVC leaves the underlying Azure Managed Disk alive in the MC_ node resource group. Step 2 specifically checks for those leftover Azure disks so you don't get billed for ghost storage.
 * Local Kubeconfig Cleanup: Once the cluster is gone, it cleans up the dead connection entry from your laptop's ~/.kube/config file.
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ==============================================================================
# Script Name: decommission-aks.sh
# Description: Safely tears down the AKS cluster, Istio mesh, Helm releases,
#              retained Persistent Volumes (Postgres/Keycloak), Static IPs,
#              Virtual Networks, and Resource Groups with interactive
#              inventory inspection and confirmation at every single step.
# ==============================================================================

set -euo pipefail

# --- Default Values (Match provision-aks.sh defaults) ---
CLUSTER_NAME="zactonics-aks"
RESOURCE_GROUP="rg-zactonics-aks"
AUTO_APPROVE=false
DRY_RUN=false

usage() {
  cat <<EOF
Usage: $(basename "$0") [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -n, --name <string>          AKS Cluster Name (default: $CLUSTER_NAME)
  -g, --resource-group <name>  Azure Resource Group (default: $RESOURCE_GROUP)
  --auto-approve               Skip interactive prompts (DANGEROUS: deletes everything!)
  --dry-run                    Show inventory at each step without deleting anything
  -h, --help                   Show this help message

Example:
  ./decommission-aks.sh -n zactonics-core-aks -g rg-zactonics-core
EOF
  exit 1
}

# --- Parse Command-Line Arguments ---
while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    -n|--name) CLUSTER_NAME="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    -g|--resource-group) RESOURCE_GROUP="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --auto-approve) AUTO_APPROVE=true; shift 1 ;;
    --dry-run) DRY_RUN=true; shift 1 ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    *) echo "Unknown parameter passed: $1"; usage ;;
  esac
done

# --- Helper Function for Step Confirmations ---
confirm_step() {
  local prompt_msg="$1"
  if [[ "$DRY_RUN" == true ]]; then
    echo "    [DRY-RUN] Would ask: '$prompt_msg' -> Skipping actual deletion."
    return 1
  fi
  if [[ "$AUTO_APPROVE" == true ]]; then
    echo "    [AUTO-APPROVE] Proceeding with: $prompt_msg"
    return 0
  fi

  while true; do
    read -r -p "👉 $prompt_msg [y/N]: " response
    case "$response" in
      [yY][eE][sS]|[yY]) return 0 ;;
      [nN][oO]|[nN]|"") echo "    ⏭️  Skipping this step."; return 1 ;;
      *) echo "    Please answer 'y' (yes) or 'n' (no)." ;;
    esac
  done
}

# --- Pre-Flight Check ---
for cmd in az kubectl helm; do
  if ! command -v "$cmd" &>/dev/null; then
    echo "[ERROR] Required command '$cmd' is not installed." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
done

echo "=================================================================="
echo " 🛑 AKS Cluster & Foundation Decommissioning Wizard"
echo "=================================================================="
echo " Target Cluster Name   : $CLUSTER_NAME"
echo " Target Resource Group : $RESOURCE_GROUP"
echo " Dry-Run Mode          : $DRY_RUN"
echo "=================================================================="

# Check if Resource Group exists at all
if ! az group exists --name "$RESOURCE_GROUP" | grep -q "true"; then
  echo "[INFO] Resource Group '$RESOURCE_GROUP' does not exist in Azure. Nothing to decommission!"
  exit 0
fi

# Check if Cluster exists and connect kubectl
CLUSTER_EXISTS=false
NODE_RG=""
if az aks show --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --name "$CLUSTER_NAME" &>/dev/null; then
  CLUSTER_EXISTS=true
  NODE_RG=$(az aks show --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --query nodeResourceGroup -o tsv)
  echo "--> Connecting kubectl to '$CLUSTER_NAME' to inspect running services..."
  az aks get-credentials --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --overwrite-existing &>/dev/null || true
else
  echo "[WARN] AKS Cluster '$CLUSTER_NAME' not found in '$RESOURCE_GROUP'. Skipping in-cluster K8s steps."
fi

# ==============================================================================
# STEP 1: Decommission In-Cluster Services, Deployments & Helm Releases
# ==============================================================================
if [[ "$CLUSTER_EXISTS" == true ]]; then
  echo ""
  echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
  echo " STEP 1 of 5: App Workloads, Services & Istio Mesh (Helm Releases)"
  echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
  echo "Checking active Helm releases across all namespaces..."
  helm list -A || true

  echo ""
  echo "Checking active Deployments, StatefulSets, and Services in target namespaces..."
  kubectl get deployments,statefulsets,svc -n data -n identity -n platform-foundation -n istio-ingress -n istio-system 2>/dev/null || true

  echo ""
  if confirm_step "Delete all foundation Helm releases, Istio Gateways, and Services shown above?"; then
    echo "    Uninstalling Helm release 'cluster-foundation'..."
    helm uninstall cluster-foundation -n platform-foundation --ignore-not-found || true

    echo "    Uninstalling Helm release 'istio-ingressgateway' (detaching Azure LoadBalancer)..."
    helm uninstall istio-ingressgateway -n istio-ingress --ignore-not-found --wait || true

    echo "    Uninstalling Helm release 'istiod' and 'istio-base'..."
    helm uninstall istiod -n istio-system --ignore-not-found || true
    helm uninstall istio-base -n istio-system --ignore-not-found || true

    echo "    Deleting application deployments/services in 'identity' and 'data' namespaces..."
    kubectl delete all --all -n identity --ignore-not-found || true
    kubectl delete all --all -n data --ignore-not-found || true
    echo "    ✅ Step 1 Complete: Services, Deployments, and Istio Mesh removed."
  fi

  # ============================================================================
  # STEP 2: Decommission Persistent Volume Claims (PVCs) & Retained Azure Disks
  # ============================================================================
  echo ""
  echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
  echo " STEP 2 of 5: Persistent Volumes (PostgreSQL & Keycloak Vaults)"
  echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
  echo "Because your volumes use 'helm.sh/resource-policy: keep' and 'Retain',"
  echo "they are protected from accidental deletion during Step 1."
  echo ""
  echo "Current PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs) and PersistentVolumes (PVs):"
  kubectl get pvc,pv -A || true

  if [[ -n "$NODE_RG" ]]; then
    echo ""
    echo "Underlying Azure Managed Disks in Node Resource Group ($NODE_RG):"
    az disk list --resource-group "$NODE_RG" --query "[].{Name:name, SizeGB:diskSizeGb, State:diskState, SKU:sku.name}" -o table || true
  fi

  echo ""
  if confirm_step "PERMANENTLY DELETE all PostgreSQL/Keycloak PVCs, PVs, and Azure Disks?"; then
    echo "    Deleting PVCs in 'data' and 'identity' namespaces..."
    kubectl delete pvc --all -n data --ignore-not-found || true
    kubectl delete pvc --all -n identity --ignore-not-found || true

    echo "    Deleting custom StorageClass 'managed-csi-premium-retain'..."
    kubectl delete storageclass managed-csi-premium-retain --ignore-not-found || true

    echo "    Deleting namespaces 'data', 'identity', 'platform-foundation', 'istio-ingress', 'istio-system'..."
    kubectl delete ns data identity platform-foundation istio-ingress istio-system --ignore-not-found || true

    if [[ -n "$NODE_RG" ]]; then
      echo "    Checking for any retained/unattached Azure Disks in '$NODE_RG'..."
      DISK_IDS=$(az disk list --resource-group "$NODE_RG" --query "[?diskState=='Unattached' || diskState=='Reserved'].id" -o tsv)
      if [[ -n "$DISK_IDS" ]]; then
        echo "    Deleting retained Azure Managed Disks..."
        az disk delete --ids $DISK_IDS --yes --output none
      fi
    fi
    echo "    ✅ Step 2 Complete: Persistent volumes and Azure disks wiped."
  fi
fi

# ==============================================================================
# STEP 3: Decommission Static Public IP for Istio Gateway
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " STEP 3 of 5: Reserved Static Public IP Address"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
PUBLIC_IP_NAME="${CLUSTER_NAME}-istio-pip"

if [[ -n "$NODE_RG" ]] && az network public-ip show --resource-group "$NODE_RG" --name "$PUBLIC_IP_NAME" &>/dev/null; then
  echo "Found Reserved Static Public IP in '$NODE_RG':"
  az network public-ip show --resource-group "$NODE_RG" --name "$PUBLIC_IP_NAME" \
    --query "{Name:name, IPAddress:ipAddress, SKU:sku.name, Allocation:publicIpAllocationMethod}" -o table

  echo ""
  if confirm_step "Release and delete Static Public IP '$PUBLIC_IP_NAME'?"; then
    az network public-ip delete --resource-group "$NODE_RG" --name "$PUBLIC_IP_NAME" --output none
    echo "    ✅ Step 3 Complete: Static Public IP released."
  fi
else
  echo "No standalone Static Public IP '$PUBLIC_IP_NAME' found (or already removed)."
fi

# ==============================================================================
# STEP 4: Decommission AKS Cluster & Node Pools
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " STEP 4 of 5: AKS Cluster & Node Pools (System + User Wings)"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
if [[ "$CLUSTER_EXISTS" == true ]]; then
  echo "Active Node Pools inside AKS Cluster '$CLUSTER_NAME':"
  az aks nodepool list --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --cluster-name "$CLUSTER_NAME" \
    --query "[].{PoolName:name, Mode:mode, VMSize:vmSize, NodeCount:count, State:provisioningState}" -o table

  echo ""
  if confirm_step "Delete the entire AKS Cluster '$CLUSTER_NAME' and its Node Pools?"; then
    echo "    Deleting AKS Cluster '$CLUSTER_NAME' (this takes a few minutes)..."
    az aks delete --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --name "$CLUSTER_NAME" --yes --output none

    echo "    Cleaning up local ~/.kube/config context for '$CLUSTER_NAME'..."
    kubectl config delete-context "$CLUSTER_NAME" &>/dev/null || true
    kubectl config delete-cluster "$CLUSTER_NAME" &>/dev/null || true
    kubectl config unset "users.clusterUser_${RESOURCE_GROUP}_${CLUSTER_NAME}" &>/dev/null || true

    echo "    ✅ Step 4 Complete: AKS Cluster destroyed and local kubeconfig cleaned."
  fi
else
  echo "AKS Cluster '$CLUSTER_NAME' is already deleted."
fi

# ==============================================================================
# STEP 5: Decommission Custom VNet & Main Azure Resource Group
# ==============================================================================
echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo " STEP 5 of 5: Virtual Network (VNet) & Azure Resource Group"
echo "------------------------------------------------------------------"
echo "Remaining Azure resources inside Resource Group '$RESOURCE_GROUP':"
az resource list --resource-group "$RESOURCE_GROUP" \
  --query "[].{Name:name, Type:type, Location:location}" -o table || true

echo ""
if confirm_step "Delete the Virtual Network and the ENTIRE Resource Group '$RESOURCE_GROUP'?"; then
  echo "    Deleting Resource Group '$RESOURCE_GROUP' and all remaining contents..."
  az group delete --name "$RESOURCE_GROUP" --yes --output none
  echo "    ✅ Step 5 Complete: Resource Group '$RESOURCE_GROUP' completely removed."
fi

echo ""
echo "=================================================================="
echo " 🏁 Decommissioning Process Finished!"
echo "=================================================================="

How to Use the Decommission Script
1. Make it executable
chmod +x decommission-aks.sh

2. Run a Safe "Practice Inspection" (--dry-run)
Want to see every service, deployment, volume, and IP address that would be deleted without actually touching a single thing in Azure? Run:
./decommission-aks.sh \
  --name "zactonics-core-aks" \
  --resource-group "rg-zactonics-core" \
  --dry-run

3. Run the Interactive Step-by-Step Teardown
When you are ready to decommission, run it normally and it will pause at each of the 5 stages, show you a live table of what is inside that stage, and ask [y/N]:
./decommission-aks.sh \
  --name "zactonics-core-aks" \
  --resource-group "rg-zactonics-core"

Pro-Tip: Partial Teardown (Saving Your Data Vaults!)
Because the script asks for your permission at every step, you can also use it to pause your cloud bill over the weekend without losing your database:
 * Step 1 (Services & Istio): Type y (shuts down traffic cleanly).
 * Step 2 (Postgres & Keycloak Volumes): Type n (keeps your disks safe!).
 * Step 3 (Static IP): Type n (keeps your IP address reserved).
 * Step 4 (AKS Cluster): Type n (or scale node pools to zero).
 * Step 5 (Resource Group): Type n.
